## 1. The idea in one paragraph

An individual learning agent can pass every stability test we know how to run
and still help destabilize the market it operates in. When adaptive agents
share an environment that reacts to their decisions - dealers whose quotes
reshape a common pool of informed flow, recommenders competing for one
attention pool, pricers facing one demand curve - each agent's retraining
changes the data every *other* agent will learn from next. That coupling is a
**learning externality**: no agent internalizes the effect of its own
adaptation on the environment its competitors face, and feedback that is
harmless in one agent compounds across many. Two questions follow, and the
paper answers both. *What sets the strength of the externality?* Not the
number of firms but the **effective number of independent learners**, a
spectral measure of how aligned the firms' feedback directions are: fifty
dealers fine-tuning one vendor's foundation model are dynamically close to
one very large learner, so foundation-model concentration enters the
stability condition as a term rather than as a talking point. *What can be
done about it?* Three closed-form levers, all expressed in that one quantity:
a **crowding-cadence frontier** saying how much slower everyone must retrain
as the market's effective crowding rises, and the crowding level past which
no cadence helps; a **herd-immunity threshold** saying the market is stable
iff the fraction of agents running feedback-aware corrected updates exceeds
`1 - 1/m_N`, exactly the epidemiological `1 - 1/R_0` law with the systemic
modulus as the reproduction number; and a **Pigouvian wedge** pricing the
externality. The synthesis result is that the last two trade off against each
other: **model diversity and corrected learning are substitutes**, and the
paper gives the frontier along which a market can buy stability with either.

## 2. Fit to the EconML call

Verbatim Theme 2 bullets hit: *"Algorithmic monoculture and model
multiplicity"*, *"Feedback loops and performative prediction effects"*,
*"Market concentration among AI service providers"*, *"AI supply chains and
their dynamics"*, *"Multi-agent learning dynamics in economic environments"*,
and *"Ecosystem-level incentive design"*. All six, with one model. Neither
predecessor draft managed that: the externality draft carried the policy
apparatus but no monoculture or supply-chain angle, and the alignment draft
carried those but spent its page budget on spectral machinery this audience
does not grade on.

The paper also carries the three things an economics-literate audience checks
for and pure-ML submissions usually lack: an explicit externality model with
a welfare statement, closed-form policy levers of all three standard types
(quantity, technology, price), and an honest empirical design with a placebo.
Its central message - individual model evaluation cannot certify a market of
models - is an economics claim argued with economics tools.

## 3. Background: the base result this builds on

REFLEX (Section 4) derives, for a structural OTC market-making model, the
performative retraining modulus `m_1 = eps*beta/gamma` of a single dealer,
every constant computed from microstructure primitives rather than fitted,
and its multi-dealer extension: `N` symmetric dealers sharing one pool of
informed flow with spillover `kappa in [0,1]` have joint retraining Jacobian

```
   J = -m_1 [ (1-kappa) I + kappa 1 1' ] ,
```

whose spectrum splits into a common mode with eigenvalue `-m_1 * N_eff`
(`N_eff = 1 + kappa(N-1)`) and differential modes with eigenvalue
`-m_1 (1-kappa)`. The common mode dominates, so the market destabilizes at

```
   m_N = N_eff * m_1 = 1 ,   i.e. at the critical population  N_c = 1/m_1
                                                              (at kappa = 1),
```

a factor `N_eff` before any individual dealer's own loop would. Measured in a
genuine shared-pool simulator: amplification `1.74x` at `N = 2` and `3.16x`
at `N = 3`, against predicted `2` and `3`. [VERIFIED - base result]

This paper takes that mechanism as its starting point and asks the economics
questions: what kind of market failure is this, what actually sets its
strength, and what are the levers?

## 4. The framing

**Private vs. systemic stability.** Two different questions: is each agent
stable considered alone (`m_1 < 1`), and is the collection stable when they
interact (`m_N < 1`)? The shared environment makes these different. Dealer A
adapts, the pool changes, dealer B observes a changed environment and adapts,
which changes the pool again, which A retrains on. Each agent behaves
rationally and passes its own stability test while the interaction amplifies
the loop. **Agent count is not a descriptive market characteristic; it enters
the stability condition.** Systemic instability is an emergent property of
the interaction structure, not a property of any agent.

**The externality reading.** Each participant does not internalize the effect
of its adaptive behavior on the environment faced by other adaptive
participants. This is why "is this model stable when deployed?" is the wrong
certification question in a shared endogenous environment, and why "is the
ecosystem of interacting models stable?" is the right one. The gap between
the two questions is precisely the externality, and closing it is what
Sections 6 through 8 are about.

**The reproduction number.** `m_N` is the **feedback reproduction number** of
the market: the factor by which a common perturbation is amplified per
retraining round. `m_N < 1` and perturbations die out; `m_N > 1` and they
compound. Everything that follows is the economics of controlling `R_0`, and
Section 5 is about what actually determines it.
